Chapter 5
Shares: common, preferred, and what they are worth
الأسهم العادية والممتازة وكيف تُقيَّم
A bond is a promise. A share is not: it is a claim on whatever is left after every promise has been kept. That difference runs through everything in this chapter — what a shareholder is entitled to, what a preferred shareholder gets instead, and why valuing a share is so much harder than valuing a bond.
The method does not change. A share is worth the present value of what it pays, which is its dividends, discounted at the return its risk requires. What changes is that the payments are neither fixed nor promised nor finite, so a growth rate has to be assumed, and the answer is only as good as the assumption. The second half of the chapter is about the alternative that practitioners reach for when the assumption will not hold: valuing a company against what the market pays for others like it.
Figure F605-01 is your map.
Figure F605-01. Chapter 5 roadmap: two kinds of share, two ways to value them.
