WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Book 1 computed Orontes's earnings per share of $0.3666, its dividend per share of $0.0556, its book value per share of $2.5177 and its market price of $9.25, and from those a price/earnings ratio of 25.23 and a market-to-book ratio of 3.67. Chapter 2 of this book put the required return on its equity at 9.4 per cent. Everything in this chapter is built from those six numbers.
5.1  Common stock
A common share is an ownership interest, and five characteristics define it.
It is a residual claim. The common shareholder is paid after employees, suppliers, lenders and preferred shareholders, in good years and in a liquidation alike. That is why equity carries the highest required return in the ladder of Chapter 1, and why it has no ceiling: the residue can be very large.
It carries limited liability. A shareholder can lose what was paid for the share and no more, which is the invention that makes public companies possible.
It carries votes. One share, one vote is the normal arrangement, used to elect directors and approve major transactions. Dual-class structures give some shares more votes than others, which lets founders keep control with a minority of the economics, and which the exam treats as a governance risk rather than a defect.
It may carry a pre-emptive right, which entitles existing holders to subscribe for new shares before outsiders, in proportion to what they hold, so that their percentage is not diluted without their consent.
It is described by a share count with four different meanings, and questions exploit the difference. Shares authorised are the maximum the charter permits. Shares issued are those ever sold. Treasury shares are issued shares the company has bought back. Shares outstanding are issued less treasury, and they are the ones that vote and receive dividends. Orontes has 8,100,000 issued and outstanding and no treasury stock, which is why Book 1 could use one figure throughout.
Par value, finally, is a legal formality in modern practice. Orontes's shares carry a $1 par value, so $8,100,000 sits in common stock and $5,400,000 in additional paid-in capital, and the split between the two columns tells a reader nothing about the company.
Figure F605-02 lists the characteristics against preferred stock, which section 5.2 develops.
Figure F605-02. Common against preferred
@table
Feature | Common stock | Preferred stock
Claim on income | Residual, after everybody | Fixed dividend, ahead of common
Claim in liquidation | Last | Ahead of common, behind all debt
Dividend | Discretionary | Fixed, and often cumulative
Votes | Normally one a share | Normally none
Upside | Unlimited | Capped at the fixed dividend
Maturity | None | None, unless callable or redeemable
@end
