5.2  Preferred stock
Preferred stock sits between debt and equity and takes characteristics from both, which is why the exam calls it a hybrid.
From debt it takes a fixed payment and priority. The dividend is stated as a percentage of par or as an amount, it does not rise when profits rise, and it must be paid in full before any common dividend. In a liquidation the preferred holder ranks ahead of the common and behind every creditor.
From equity it takes discretion and permanence. The dividend is declared, not owed: missing it is not a default and cannot force the company into bankruptcy. There is usually no maturity. And the payment is not deductible for tax, which Chapter 4 showed is the decisive economic difference from debt: a 9 per cent preferred costs 9 per cent after tax, while a 9 per cent bond costs 6.75.
The variants are examinable by name. Cumulative preferred carries unpaid dividends forward as arrears that block the common dividend; non-cumulative does not, and is correspondingly cheaper for the issuer and worse for the holder. Participating preferred takes its fixed dividend and then shares in further profits. Convertible preferred can be exchanged for common shares. Callable or redeemable preferred can be bought back by the issuer at a stated price, which caps the holder's gain.
Valuation is the perpetuity of Chapter 4: the dividend over the required return. A $100 par 9 per cent preferred pays $9.00 a year, and if the market requires 10 per cent for that risk it is worth 9.00 ÷ 0.10 = 90.00. If the market requires 8 per cent it is worth 9.00 ÷ 0.08 = 112.50.
Why issue it at all, given that it costs more after tax than debt? Three reasons the exam accepts. It carries no covenants and no default risk, so it does not restrict the company or threaten it. Rating agencies and lenders treat part of it as equity, so it preserves borrowing capacity. And it raises money without giving away votes, which matters to a controlling family — a consideration that will decide Barada's financing question later in this book.
Figure F605-03 prices the variants.
Figure F605-03. What each preferred feature does
@table
Feature | Who it favours | Effect on the rate the issuer must offer
Cumulative | The holder | Lower
Participating | The holder | Lower
Convertible | The holder | Much lower
Callable | The issuer | Higher
Non-cumulative | The issuer | Higher
@end
EXAM TRAP   Preferred is not debt
A missed preferred dividend is not a default and creates no liability on the balance sheet. It creates arrears, disclosed in the notes, which block the common dividend.
Preferred dividends are deducted from net income before earnings per share, and preferred stock is deducted from equity before book value per common share.
The preferred dividend is not deductible for tax. Comparing a 9 per cent preferred with a 9 per cent bond without adjusting for tax is the standard error.
