5.4  The two-stage model
Real companies grow fast for a while and then slow down, as competition arrives and the market fills. The two-stage model says so explicitly: a period of high growth with each dividend discounted individually, then a constant-growth phase valued by the formula of section 5.3 and discounted back as a single terminal value.
WORKED EXAMPLE   Worked example: two stages
Step 1. The assumptions. A company has just paid a dividend of $2.00. It will grow at 15 per cent for two years and at 5 per cent for ever after. The required return is 10 per cent.
Step 2. The explicit dividends. Year one: 2.00 × 1.15 = 2.30. Year two: 2.30 × 1.15 = 2.6450. Year three, the first of the slow phase: 2.6450 × 1.05 = 2.7773.
Step 3. The terminal value. At the end of year two the remaining stream is a constant-growth perpetuity worth 2.7773 ÷ 0.05 = 55.546.
Step 4. Discount everything to today. Year one: 2.30 ÷ 1.10 = 2.0909. Year two carries both the dividend and the terminal value: 2.6450 + 55.546 = 58.191, and 58.191 ÷ 1.21 = 48.09. The share is worth 2.0909 + 48.09 = 50.18.
Check. Note where the value sits: more than ninety-five per cent of it is in the terminal value, which rests entirely on an assumed 5 per cent for ever. That is true of almost every two-stage valuation and it is the first thing to say when presenting one. The high-growth phase that the analysis spent all its effort forecasting contributed four per cent of the answer.
Figure F605-05 sets the computation out.
Figure F605-05. The two-stage model, step by step
@table
Year | Dividend | Discount factor at 10% | Present value
1 | 2.3000 | 0.9091 | 2.0909
2 | 2.6450 | 0.8264 | 2.1860
Terminal value at end of year 2 | 55.546 | 0.8264 | 45.904
Value a share | | | 50.18
@end
The same logic extends. A three-stage model adds a transition phase in which growth declines gradually, which is more realistic and adds no new idea. Where a company pays no dividend at all, the same machinery is applied to free cash flow instead, which Book 3 develops for capital projects and acquisitions.
