5.5  Relative valuation
The alternative to discounting is comparison: value a company at what the market pays for similar companies, using a multiple of something the companies share.
The price/earnings ratio is the common one. Take the multiple at which comparable companies trade, multiply by the subject company's earnings per share, and that is the implied value. If food manufacturers of Orontes's size trade at 18 times earnings, Orontes's implied value is 18 × 0.3666 = 6.60 a share, against a market price of $9.25. Either the market thinks Orontes is better than its peers, or it is dear.
The market-to-book ratio compares price with net assets. It is most useful where assets are carried near their current values, which is why it is the standard multiple for banks and insurers and a weak one for a company whose value is in brands and recipes. Orontes's is 3.67.
The price/sales ratio divides market capitalisation by revenue: 74,925,000 ÷ 42,000,000 = 1.78 for Orontes. Its use is the case the other two cannot handle — a company with no earnings, or with earnings distorted by a one-off such as the $2,724,000 impairment Book 1 spent a section on. Sales are harder to manipulate than earnings, which is its second attraction, and it ignores profitability entirely, which is its fatal weakness: two companies with the same sales and different margins are not worth the same.
Figure F605-06 compares the three.
Figure F605-06. Three multiples, and when each is the right one
@table
Multiple | Orontes | Best used for | Main weakness
Price/earnings | 25.23 | Profitable companies with comparable accounting | Earnings can be distorted or negative
Market/book | 3.67 | Banks, insurers, asset-heavy businesses | Ignores unrecognised intangibles
Price/sales | 1.78 | Loss-making or early-stage companies | Ignores profitability entirely
@end
Three rules govern the method and all three are examined. The comparables must be genuinely comparable in business, size, growth and risk — Book 1's warning about Barada's 14 per cent gross margin against Orontes's 35 is the same point. The accounting must be on the same basis, or the multiples are not measuring the same thing. And relative valuation tells you what the market pays today, not what a company is worth: a sector valued at thirty times earnings gives a high answer for every company in it, and the method has no way of saying the sector is wrong.
