LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Equity"
English equity means three different things in these books. It is the owners' claim on a company, as in stockholders' equity. It is the shares themselves, as in an equity investment. And in ordinary English it means fairness, which is where the word came from and why courts of equity have nothing to do with shares. Arabic uses حقوق الملكية for the ownership claim and أسهم for the instruments, which keeps the first two apart. French uses capitaux propres for the balance sheet claim and actions for the instruments, and équité only for fairness — the cleanest separation of the three languages. The exam uses the first two senses interchangeably and expects you to tell them apart from the context; the phrase cost of equity, for instance, means the return required on the ownership claim, not the price of a share.
