Case-style practice: an offer for Barada
The private investor of Book 1, Chapter 9 values Barada Wholesale's equity at $6,000,000 for a 25 per cent stake. Barada has 1,000,000 shares, book equity of $3,100,000, net income of $283,500, sales of $21,978,000 and pays no dividend. Distributors of its size trade at about 14 times earnings and 1.2 times book. Barada's required return on equity, from its beta of 1.30, is 10.8 per cent.
Required. Value Barada's shares three ways and say what the investor's offer implies. Name the single figure you would want before advising the owner to accept. Under 250 words.
Answers and explanations are at the end of the chapter.
Answers and explanations
Section checks
SC5-1  Answer A. Common equity is paid last.
B is wrong. Lenders rank ahead of shareholders.
C is wrong. The residual is by definition not fixed.
D is wrong. Nothing about a share is guaranteed.
SC5-2  Answer A. Treasury shares are issued but not outstanding.
B is wrong. Authorised shares may never have been issued.
C is wrong. Treasury shares are deducted, not added.
D is wrong. That is the unissued balance.
SC5-3  Answer A. The right preserves an existing holder's proportion.
B is wrong. No shareholder can demand a dividend.
C is wrong. Votes come from the shares themselves.
D is wrong. That would be a put right, which common shares do not carry.
SC5-4  Answer A. The dividend is 9.00 and 9.00 ÷ 0.10 = 90.00.
B is wrong. Par is the base for the dividend, not the value.
C is wrong. 112.50 is the value at a required 8 per cent.
D is wrong. That is one year's dividend.
SC5-5  Answer A. It is not a default; cumulative stock accrues arrears.
B is wrong. Only a debt payment creates a default.
C is wrong. An undeclared dividend is not a liability.
D is wrong. Companies miss preferred dividends in bad years.
SC5-6  Answer A. The dividend is paid from taxed profit.
B is wrong. The rate in the comparison is the same.
C is wrong. Preferred ranks behind debt.
D is wrong. Preferred normally carries no votes.
SC5-7  Answer A. Next year's dividend over the required return less growth.
B is wrong. That is the perpetuity with no growth.
C is wrong. Earnings are not dividends and growth is not a discount rate.
D is wrong. Book value plays no part in the model.
SC5-8  Answer A. The denominator becomes zero or negative.
B is wrong. Low growth gives a low value, not an impossible one.
C is wrong. Zero growth gives the simple perpetuity.
D is wrong. Negative growth is handled normally.
SC5-9  Answer A. 9.4 − 0.62 = 8.78 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the dividend yield.
C is wrong. Four per cent is the assumption the model started from and the price rejects.
D is wrong. 13.70 per cent is the sustainable growth rate, which exceeds the required return.
SC5-10  Answer A. It is the constant-growth value of everything after the explicit period.
B is wrong. The final explicit dividend is one input to it.
C is wrong. Book value plays no part.
D is wrong. The explicit dividends are discounted separately.
SC5-11  Answer A. Typically well over three-quarters.
B is wrong. The terminal value dominates.
C is wrong. There is no such rule.
D is wrong. It is the largest component.
SC5-12  Answer A. 18 × 0.3666 = 6.60.
B is wrong. 9.25 is the market price, not the implied value.
C is wrong. 18 is the multiple.
D is wrong. This divides rather than multiplies.
SC5-13  Answer A. Sales exist where earnings do not.
B is wrong. A bank is valued on book.
C is wrong. The multiple needs sales to work.
D is wrong. It ignores profitability, so it is a last resort.
SC5-14  Answer A. It prices against the market, not against value.
B is wrong. It is the easiest of all to compute.
C is wrong. No dividend is needed.
D is wrong. The share price is the numerator.
