Item | Answer
Earnings per share | 0.2835: 283,500 ÷ 1,000,000.
Book value per share | 3.10: 3,100,000 ÷ 1,000,000.
Constant growth value | 1.32: next year's dividend is 0.10 × 1.03 = 0.103, and 0.103 ÷ 0.078 = 1.32.
Value at a peer multiple | 3.97: 14 × 0.2835 = 3.97.
Value at a peer market-to-book | 3.72: 1.2 × 3.10 = 3.72.
Price/sales at 6.00 a share | 0.27: the market capitalisation is 1,000,000 × 6.00 = 6,000,000, and 6,000,000 ÷ 21,978,000 = 0.27.
What it tells you | Three of the four methods put Barada between about 3.70 and 4.00 a share, and the investor is offering 6.00. On these figures the offer is generous, not mean — which is the opposite of what the book value of 3.10 alone suggests, and the reason the owner should take advice before negotiating on book value.
